// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · WIRING / DEPENDENCY INJECTION
//
// The only piece here that can make the call graph SMALLER rather than larger —
// and, measured, the one whose payoff depends entirely on the codebase (see
// MEASURED below before quoting this file as a precision win).
//
// THE CLAIM. An injection point typed as an interface currently fans to every
// implementor (multi_inferred) — that is what resolution/virtual-dispatch.dl means
// by "narrowing to the single injected implementation is a data-flow / points-to
// concern for a later engine". This is that later engine, for the one case a
// container makes decidable: if exactly ONE bean satisfies the injection point,
// the container will inject that bean, so the receiver's runtime type is known and
// the CHA fan is wrong, not merely imprecise.
//
// MEASURED — the claim above holds on constructed cases and FAILS TO PAY at scale.
// Recorded here because a claim this file opens with must carry its own evidence.
//
//   test/java/cases/25-di-narrowing, 4 interface-typed call sites:
//       CHA only   12 targets (3 per site)
//       with DI     6 targets — three sites collapse to known_edge, one keeps its
//                   full fan (the control: two beans satisfy it, so narrowing it
//                   would be a guess).
//
//   spring-cloud-config-server (276 files, 40,649 LOC), narrowing ON vs OFF —
//   toggled with the cfg_di_narrowing_enabled() fact in knobs.dl:
//       known_edge 3,990 and multi_inferred 2,781 in BOTH runs. ZERO edges changed.
//
// The bean/DI model is not what failed: it found 169 beans, 96 injection points and
// 45 uniquely-resolved ones. What failed is the POPULATION — where those injection
// points point:
//       57  client CONCRETE class (44 uniquely resolved) — no fan existed to remove
//       23  client INTERFACE                             — but only 1 resolves to a
//                                                          single bean; 164 di_edge
//                                                          rows are multi_bean
//       16  library / unknown type                       — gate (1) blocks it, and
//                                                          all 16 are unsatisfied
// So the population the narrowing exists for — an interface-typed injection point
// with exactly one candidate bean — is 1 of 96 here. A Spring Cloud module injects
// FRAMEWORK interfaces whose implementations are contributed by
// spring-boot-autoconfigure's own @Configuration classes, which are not in the
// scanned set, so those points read unsatisfied or multi_bean instead of resolving.
//
// WHAT THIS POINTED AT, AND WHAT #851 DID ABOUT IT. This paragraph used to read
// "every rule in this folder is scoped to client", which was the defect: the
// lib_annotation / lib_annotation_argument relations were staged (as of #136) and
// carried the framework's own stereotypes and @Bean methods, and nothing read them.
// annotation-args.dl now threads a provenance column, so a dependency supplied with
// --library contributes bean_def, inject_point and di_edge rows like any other source.
//
// The NARROWING is a separate question and #851 deliberately left it alone. Gate (1)
// below is still cfg_client_type, so a library-typed slot is still not narrowed, and
// no call edge moved. The 16 unsatisfied and 164 multi_bean rows above were measured
// before library beans were visible; whether narrowing should now widen to
// "client, or a library type from a root that was actually supplied" is open and needs
// its own soundness argument and its own numbers.
//
// The narrowing itself is correct and validated (live-context precision/recall 1.000
// across beans, field, constructor, @Bean-parameter and @Value injection: see
// test/java/expected/26-spring-oracle.spring-oracle).
//
// HOW THE NARROWING ATTACHES. Nothing here edits virtual-dispatch.dl or
// callee-resolution.dl. resolution/type-flow.dl already established the hook:
//     field_mono_in_type(OwnerType, FieldName, ConcreteType)
//        -> expr_type      (the concrete method becomes findable)
//        -> expr_exact_type (call_exact_receiver suppresses the CHA fan)
// A DI-resolved field is monomorphic for exactly the reason a `final` field with a
// `new` initializer is: the language/container guarantees one runtime type. So this
// file contributes clauses to field_mono_in_type and stops. Same mechanism, new
// evidence source.
//
// STRATIFICATION. field_mono_in_type feeds expr_exact_type, which is NEGATED by
// the CHA-fan rule. Everything reaching it must therefore be LOW — base facts,
// type_ancestor, and the declared-type resolvers (field/param/return_type_resolves,
// all built on type_ref_resolves). Nothing here reads expr_type, expr_call_candidate
// or call_chain_edge. The impact closure that DOES read call_chain_edge lives in
// impact.dl and feeds nothing back.
//
// SOUNDNESS OF THE NARROWING — stated, not assumed. Three gates:
//   (1) the declared type is a CLIENT type. A library-typed injection point can be
//       satisfied by a library/auto-configuration bean this analysis never sees, so
//       "exactly one client bean" would not mean "exactly one bean".
//   (2) exactly one bean satisfies it, AFTER any @Qualifier filter.
//   (3) the field is not also initialised with `new` (no competing evidence).
// The residual assumption is that the bean set is complete for client-typed points:
// stereotype + @Bean + XML <bean>. Programmatic registration (registerBean,
// BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor) is NOT modelled and is the one construct
// that can make this over-narrow. Turn the narrowing off by commenting out the
// single cfg_di_narrowing_enabled() fact in knobs.dl; every REPORTING relation
// (di_edge, bean_def, …) is unaffected by that switch.
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 0. bean-name helper: Spring's Introspector.decapitalize
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The default bean name is the decapitalized simple class name — UNLESS the first
// two characters are both uppercase, in which case the name is left alone (so
// `URLHandler` stays `URLHandler`, not `uRLHandler`). Reproducing it exactly is
// what makes bean_def comparable, name for name, against a live context's
// getBeanDefinitionNames() — the runtime oracle.
cfg_lower("A","a"). cfg_lower("B","b"). cfg_lower("C","c"). cfg_lower("D","d").
cfg_lower("E","e"). cfg_lower("F","f"). cfg_lower("G","g"). cfg_lower("H","h").
cfg_lower("I","i"). cfg_lower("J","j"). cfg_lower("K","k"). cfg_lower("L","l").
cfg_lower("M","m"). cfg_lower("N","n"). cfg_lower("O","o"). cfg_lower("P","p").
cfg_lower("Q","q"). cfg_lower("R","r"). cfg_lower("S","s"). cfg_lower("T","t").
cfg_lower("U","u"). cfg_lower("V","v"). cfg_lower("W","w"). cfg_lower("X","x").
cfg_lower("Y","y"). cfg_lower("Z","z").
cfg_upper_at(s, i) :- cfg_decap_demand(s), strlen(s) > i, i = range(0, 2),
    cfg_lower(substr(s, i, 1), _).
cfg_two_caps(s) :- cfg_upper_at(s, 0), cfg_upper_at(s, 1).
cfg_decap(s, s) :- cfg_decap_demand(s), cfg_two_caps(s).
cfg_decap(s, s) :- cfg_decap_demand(s), strlen(s) > 0, !cfg_upper_at(s, 0).
// A single leading capital is lowercased — Introspector.decapitalize("Foo") is
// "foo". There is deliberately NO separate one-character clause: adding one made a
// 1-char type name produce BOTH "A" and "a", i.e. two bean names for one bean.
cfg_decap(s, d) :- cfg_decap_demand(s), cfg_upper_at(s, 0), !cfg_two_caps(s),
    cfg_lower(substr(s, 0, 1), lo),
    d = cat(lo, substr(s, 1, strlen(s) - 1)).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. BEAN DEFINITIONS  —  what the container can inject
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// bean_def(BeanName, TypeHash, Mechanism, Site)
//   Mechanism ∈ { stereotype, factory_method, xml }
//   Site      the annotation / method / xml element that declares it

// (a) STEREOTYPE on a type. An explicit name — @Service("orders") — wins; otherwise
// the decapitalized simple name, matching Spring's generator.
cfg_stereotype(prov, a, t) :- ann_on_type(prov, a, n, t), cfg_bean_ann(n).
cfg_stereotype_named(t, name) :- cfg_stereotype(prov, a, t), ann_arg_value(prov, a, name), name != "".
cfg_stereotype_has_name(t) :- cfg_stereotype_named(t, _).
cfg_decap_demand(sn) :- cfg_stereotype(prov, _, t), type_decl(prov, sn, _, _, _, _, t).

bean_def(name, t, "stereotype", a) :- cfg_stereotype(_, a, t), cfg_stereotype_named(t, name).
bean_def(name, t, "stereotype", a) :- cfg_stereotype(prov, a, t), !cfg_stereotype_has_name(t),
    type_decl(prov, sn, _, _, _, _, t), cfg_decap(sn, name).

// (b) FACTORY METHOD: @Bean / @Produces / @Provides. The bean's type is the
// method's DECLARED RETURN type; its name is the method name unless given.
cfg_factory(prov, a, m, owner) :- ann_on_method(prov, a, n, m, owner), cfg_factory_ann(n).
cfg_factory_named(m, name) :- cfg_factory(prov, a, m, _), ann_arg_value(prov, a, name), name != "".
cfg_factory_has_name(m) :- cfg_factory_named(m, _).
// The bean's TYPE is the method's DECLARED return type — EXCEPT when the body
// returns a single `new T()`, in which case T is the object the container actually
// holds. Measured against a live context: reporting the declared type scored
// beans precision/recall 0.818 (`clock` typed Clock, not SystemClock); preferring
// the concrete return takes it to 1.000. method_return_mono (expr-type.dl) is the
// existing relation for exactly this and is LOW-stratum, so it is safe to read here.
// method_return_mono needs a BODY, which a library staged as signatures only does not
// have, so the concrete-return preference simply does not fire for a library factory and
// the declared return type stands. That is the correct answer for a signature, not a
// degradation: there is no `new` to read.
cfg_factory_type(m, t) :- cfg_factory(prov, _, m, _), method_return_mono(prov, m, t).
cfg_factory_type(m, t) :- cfg_factory(prov, _, m, _), !method_return_mono(prov, m, _),
    method_return_type_resolves(prov, m, _, t).
bean_def(name, t, "factory_method", m) :- cfg_factory_type(m, t), cfg_factory_named(m, name).
bean_def(name, t, "factory_method", m) :- cfg_factory_type(m, t), !cfg_factory_has_name(m),
    method_decl(_, name, _, _, m).

// (b\') PROXY REPOSITORIES: an interface whose base is a repository base.
// Spring Data, and everything shaped like it, registers a proxy for an INTERFACE the
// application declares and never implements. There is no class in any source tree and
// no annotation on the interface, so both of the things that make a bean above are
// absent and every injection of one reads `unsatisfied`.
//
// Matched on the extends-clause NAME, not on a resolved ancestor. That is the whole
// point: the base lives in the persistence framework, which is normally NOT passed as
// --library, so type_parent never fires for it and type_ancestor has no edge to match
// on. An earlier draft of this rule joined type_ancestor, passed its fixture because
// the fixture vendored the base into lib-src, and moved nothing at all on a real
// service. The name is the only evidence present in the common case.
//
// Interfaces only. A CLASS extending a repository base is ordinary code the container
// does not proxy, and a stereotype on it would already have made it a bean.
cfg_repo_super_name(sub, tn) :-
    java_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", tn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _).
cfg_repo_super_name(sub, tn) :-
    java_type_reference(_, "IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", tn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _).

cfg_repo_name_matches(tn) :- cfg_repo_super_name(_, tn), cfg_repository_base_suffix(suffix),
    strlen(tn) >= strlen(suffix),
    substr(tn, strlen(tn) - strlen(suffix), strlen(suffix)) = suffix.

// Direct: `interface OrderRepository extends MongoRepository<Order, String>`.
cfg_repo_iface(t) :- cfg_repo_super_name(t, tn), cfg_repo_name_matches(tn),
    type_decl(_, _, _, _, "INTERFACE_TYPE", _, t).
// Transitive, through a base the project declares itself: a house
// `interface BaseRepo<T> extends MongoRepository<T, String>` with per-aggregate
// children. The intermediate resolves, so type_ancestor carries that hop.
cfg_repo_iface(t) :- cfg_repo_iface(mid), type_ancestor(t, mid),
    type_decl(_, _, _, _, "INTERFACE_TYPE", _, t).

// A base marked @NoRepositoryBean is a shared supertype, not a bean: the framework
// skips it and proxies only its children. Without this the house base above is
// reported as an injectable bean that does not exist at runtime, and any interface
// typed slot it fits gains a candidate the container never supplies.
cfg_repo_excluded(t) :- ann_on_type(_, _, n, t), cfg_no_repository_ann(n).

cfg_decap_demand(sn) :- cfg_repo_iface(t), type_decl(_, sn, _, _, _, _, t).
bean_def(name, t, "repository_proxy", t) :- cfg_repo_iface(t), !cfg_repo_excluded(t),
    type_decl(_, sn, _, _, _, _, t), cfg_decap(sn, name).

// (c) XML <bean class="…"/>  (xml-wiring.dl)
bean_def(id, t, "xml", elem) :- xml_bean(elem, id, t, _).

// bean_type_is_client(BeanType) — gate (1) of the narrowing, applied on the
// injection point's declared type below.
cfg_client_type(t) :- java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// ── QUALIFIERS: the name a site may demand ───────────────────────────────────
// A bean answers to its own name, and to any @Qualifier on its type.
bean_qualifier(name, name) :- bean_def(name, _, _, _).
bean_qualifier(name, q) :- bean_def(name, t, "stereotype", _),
    ann_on_type(prov, a, an, t), cfg_qualifier_ann(an), ann_arg_value(prov, a, q), q != "".

// ── PRIMARY: the tie-break the container applies before declaring an ambiguity ─
bean_primary(name) :- bean_def(name, t, "stereotype", _),
    ann_on_type(_, _, an, t), cfg_primary_ann(an).
bean_primary(name) :- bean_def(name, _, "factory_method", m),
    ann_on_method(_, _, an, m, _), cfg_primary_ann(an).
bean_primary(name) :- bean_def(name, _, "xml", elem),
    xml_attr("client", "primary", "true", elem, _).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. INJECTION POINTS  —  what asks for a bean
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// inject_point(SiteKind, Site, DeclaredType, OwnerType, Method)
//   SiteKind ∈ { field, ctor_param, setter_param, factory_param, xml_property,
//                xml_constructor_arg }
//   Method   the method that receives it, "-" for a field.

// (a) FIELD injection: @Autowired / @Inject / @Resource on a field. @Value is
// excluded — it asks for a config KEY, not a bean, and is handled in impact.dl.
cfg_inject_field(f, owner) :- ann_on_field(_, _, n, f, owner), cfg_inject_ann(n), !cfg_value_ann(n).
inject_point("field", f, dt, owner, "-") :- cfg_inject_field(f, owner),
    field_type_resolves(_, f, _, dt).

// (b) CONSTRUCTOR injection, explicit: @Autowired/@Inject on a constructor.
cfg_inject_ctor(m, owner) :- ann_on_method(prov, _, n, m, owner), cfg_inject_ann(n), !cfg_value_ann(n),
    method_kind(prov, "CONSTRUCTOR", _, m).
// (b') CONSTRUCTOR injection, IMPLICIT (Spring 4.3+): a bean class with exactly one
// constructor, and that constructor takes arguments, is autowired without any
// annotation. This is the dominant modern idiom; omitting it would make the whole
// mechanism score near-zero recall on any recent codebase.
//
// ONLY for beans the container CONSTRUCTS. A class that is a bean because a @Bean
// factory method RETURNS it was built by that method's own `new` — the container
// never resolved its constructor arguments, so treating its parameters as injection
// points invents a resolution that did not happen. Caught by the live-context oracle:
// LoggingAuditSink (returned by AppConfig#auditSink) was reported as
// constructor-injected and Spring disagreed — ctor_inject precision 0.500 -> 1.000.
cfg_container_constructed(t) :- bean_def(_, t, "stereotype", _).
cfg_container_constructed(t) :- bean_def(_, t, "xml", _).
cfg_type_ctor(t, m) :- method_kind(prov, "CONSTRUCTOR", _, m), method_owner(prov, t, m).
cfg_type_ctor_count(t, n) :- cfg_container_constructed(t), n = count : { cfg_type_ctor(t, _) }.
cfg_inject_ctor(m, t) :- cfg_type_ctor_count(t, 1), cfg_type_ctor(t, m),
    method_kind(_, "CONSTRUCTOR", pc, m), pc != "0".
inject_point("ctor_param", p, dt, owner, m) :- cfg_inject_ctor(m, owner),
    param_decl(_, _, _, _, m, p), method_param_type_resolves(_, p, _, dt),
    !cfg_value_param(p).

// (c) SETTER / method injection: @Autowired on a non-constructor method.
cfg_inject_setter(m, owner) :- ann_on_method(prov, _, n, m, owner), cfg_inject_ann(n), !cfg_value_ann(n),
    method_kind(prov, k, _, m), k != "CONSTRUCTOR".
inject_point("setter_param", p, dt, owner, m) :- cfg_inject_setter(m, owner),
    param_decl(_, _, _, _, m, p), method_param_type_resolves(_, p, _, dt),
    !cfg_value_param(p).

// (d) FACTORY-METHOD parameters: a @Bean method's parameters are resolved from the
// context exactly like constructor arguments.
inject_point("factory_param", p, dt, owner, m) :- cfg_factory(_, _, m, owner),
    param_decl(_, _, _, _, m, p), method_param_type_resolves(_, p, _, dt),
    !cfg_value_param(p).

// A parameter carrying @Value is a config-key injection, not a bean injection.
cfg_value_param(p) :- ann_on_param(_, _, n, p, _), cfg_value_ann(n).

// (e) XML wiring is NOT an inject_point: <property ref="other"/> names its target
// bean DIRECTLY, so there is no type matching to do and no candidate set to count.
// It goes straight to di_edge below, resolved or dangling.

// ── explicit qualifier on a site ─────────────────────────────────────────────
inject_qualifier(f, q) :- cfg_inject_field(f, _), ann_on_field(prov, a, n, f, _),
    cfg_qualifier_ann(n), ann_arg_value(prov, a, q), q != "".
inject_qualifier(p, q) :- ann_on_param(prov, a, n, p, _), cfg_qualifier_ann(n),
    ann_arg_value(prov, a, q), q != "".
inject_has_qualifier(s) :- inject_qualifier(s, _).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. SATISFACTION  —  which beans could land in this slot
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A bean satisfies a point if its type IS the declared type or a subtype of it.
// type_ancestor is not reflexive, hence the two clauses.
cfg_bean_fits(site, name, bt) :- inject_point(_, site, dt, _, _), dt != "-",
    bean_def(name, bt, _, _), bt = dt.
cfg_bean_fits(site, name, bt) :- inject_point(_, site, dt, _, _), dt != "-",
    bean_def(name, bt, _, _), type_ancestor(bt, dt).
// A @Qualifier narrows the candidate set to beans answering to that name; it
// OUTRANKS @Primary, exactly as the container orders them.
bean_satisfies(site, name, bt) :- cfg_bean_fits(site, name, bt),
    inject_qualifier(site, q), bean_qualifier(name, q).
// No qualifier: every fitting bean, UNLESS exactly one of them is @Primary — in
// which case the container picks that one deterministically and so do we. Two
// primaries is a startup error in Spring, so "exactly one" is the only case where
// the rank decides anything; with 0 or 2+, the full candidate set stands.
cfg_site_primary(site, name) :- cfg_bean_fits(site, name, _), bean_primary(name).
cfg_site_primary_count(site, n) :- inject_point(_, site, _, _, _),
    n = count : { cfg_site_primary(site, _) }.
cfg_site_primary_unique(site) :- cfg_site_primary_count(site, 1).
bean_satisfies(site, name, bt) :- cfg_bean_fits(site, name, bt),
    !inject_has_qualifier(site), !cfg_site_primary_unique(site).
bean_satisfies(site, name, bt) :- cfg_bean_fits(site, name, bt),
    !inject_has_qualifier(site), cfg_site_primary_unique(site), bean_primary(name).

// Count DISTINCT bean TYPES, not bean names: two names for one type (an alias, or a
// stereotype whose class is also declared in XML) is still one injectable object,
// and counting names would refuse to narrow a case the container resolves fine.
cfg_satisfying_type(site, bt) :- bean_satisfies(site, _, bt).
inject_bean_count(site, n) :- inject_point(_, site, _, _, _),
    n = count : { cfg_satisfying_type(site, _) }.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. di_edge  —  THE EXPORT
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// di_edge(Site, SiteKind, DeclaredType, BeanName, BeanType, Status)
//   Status ∈ { known_bean, multi_bean, unsatisfied, xml_ref, xml_ref_dangling }
// Deliberately mirrors call_chain.dl's vocabulary: one resolved target is a known
// edge, several is a sound fan, none is a DECLARED dead end that is never dropped.
di_edge(site, kind, dt, name, bt, "known_bean") :- inject_point(kind, site, dt, _, _),
    inject_bean_count(site, 1), bean_satisfies(site, name, bt).
di_edge(site, kind, dt, name, bt, "multi_bean") :- inject_point(kind, site, dt, _, _),
    inject_bean_count(site, n), n >= 2, bean_satisfies(site, name, bt).
di_edge(site, kind, dt, "-", "-", "unsatisfied") :- inject_point(kind, site, dt, _, _),
    dt != "-", inject_bean_count(site, 0).
config_unresolved("di", site, dt, "unsatisfied_injection_point") :-
    inject_point(_, site, dt, _, _), dt != "-", inject_bean_count(site, 0).

// XML wiring resolves by NAME, so it needs no type matching — but a dangling ref is
// a defect worth surfacing.
di_edge(wireElem, wireKind, "-", refId, bt, "xml_ref") :-
    xml_bean(ownerElem, _, _, _), xml_wire(ownerElem, wireElem, wireKind, _, refId),
    bean_def(refId, bt, _, _).
cfg_xml_ref_resolved(refId) :- bean_def(refId, _, _, _).
di_edge(wireElem, wireKind, "-", refId, "-", "xml_ref_dangling") :-
    xml_bean(ownerElem, _, _, _), xml_wire(ownerElem, wireElem, wireKind, _, refId),
    !cfg_xml_ref_resolved(refId).
config_unresolved("xml", wireElem, refId, "dangling_bean_ref") :-
    xml_bean(ownerElem, _, _, _), xml_wire(ownerElem, wireElem, _, _, refId),
    !cfg_xml_ref_resolved(refId).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. THE NARROWING  —  where the graph gets smaller
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// di_field_mono(Field, ConcreteType): this field provably holds one runtime type.
//
// (a) FIELD injection with a unique bean.
di_field_mono(f, bt) :- cfg_di_narrowing_enabled(),
    inject_point("field", f, dt, _, _),
    cfg_client_type(dt),                       // gate (1): client-typed slot
    inject_bean_count(f, 1),                   // gate (2): exactly one bean
    bean_satisfies(f, _, bt),
    !field_init_new_type(f, _).                // gate (3): no competing `new`

// (b) CONSTRUCTOR injection into a FINAL field — `private final H h; Ctor(H h){...}`.
// `final` is what makes this sound: the field cannot be reassigned, so if the only
// value that can reach it is the injected one, its runtime type is the bean's. The
// constructor parameter is matched BY TYPE, which is how the container matches it.
cfg_ctor_injected_field(f, dt) :- field_final(f),
    field_decl("client", _, _, owner, f),
    field_type_resolves(_, f, _, dt),
    cfg_inject_ctor(m, owner),
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, m, p),
    method_param_type_resolves(_, p, _, dt),
    !cfg_value_param(p).
di_field_mono(f, bt) :- cfg_di_narrowing_enabled(),
    cfg_ctor_injected_field(f, dt),
    cfg_client_type(dt),
    inject_point("ctor_param", p, dt, _, _), inject_bean_count(p, 1),
    bean_satisfies(p, _, bt),
    !field_init_new_type(f, _).

// Refuse to narrow if two different concrete types were derived for one field —
// contradictory evidence must widen, never pick.
di_field_multi(f) :- di_field_mono(f, t1), di_field_mono(f, t2), t1 != t2.

// ── THE HOOK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Contributing to field_mono_in_type (resolution/type-flow.dl) rather than
// duplicating its six expr_type / expr_exact_type clauses: a DI-resolved field is
// monomorphic in exactly the sense that file already defines, so it should enter
// through the same door. Every consumer — qualified receiver, implicit-this
// receiver, ancestor lookup, fan suppression — then works with no further change.
field_mono_in_type(owner, name, t) :- di_field_mono(f, t), !di_field_multi(f),
    java_field(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, owner, _, _, _, _, f).

// ── the OTHER receiver spelling ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// field_mono_in_type is consumed by resolution/type-flow.dl only for FIELD_ACCESS
// nodes — `this.store.read()`. A BARE `store.read()` is emitted by the parser as
// IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE / edgeRole RECEIVER / referencedEntityKind FIELD, which
// those clauses never match, so the narrowing would silently do nothing for the
// single most common way a field receiver is written. (The same hole affects the
// pre-existing `final Foo f = new FooImpl()` narrowing; it is left alone here
// rather than widened, so no existing edge changes.)
//
// These clauses are DI-scoped on purpose: they key on di_field_in_type, not on
// field_mono_in_type, so a project with no container annotations derives nothing
// from them and every existing golden is untouched.
di_field_in_type(owner, name, t) :- di_field_mono(f, t), !di_field_multi(f),
    java_field(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, owner, _, _, _, _, f).

// Shadow guard: a local or parameter of the same name in the same method is NOT
// the field, whatever the parser stamped. Reads containment + base facts only, so
// it stays in the low stratum that expr_exact_type requires.
di_recv_shadowed(e) :- java_expression("IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", "RECEIVER", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, name, _, _, _, "FIELD", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", e, m),
    java_local_variable(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m, _, _, _, _, _).
di_recv_shadowed(e) :- java_expression("IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", "RECEIVER", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, name, _, _, _, "FIELD", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", e, m),
    java_method_parameter(name, _, m, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _).

di_recv_mono(e, t) :- java_expression("IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", "RECEIVER", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, name, _, _, _, "FIELD", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e),
    !di_recv_shadowed(e),
    expr_ultimate_type("client", e, encl),
    di_field_in_type(encl, name, t).
di_recv_mono(e, t) :- java_expression("IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", "RECEIVER", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, name, _, _, _, "FIELD", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e),
    !di_recv_shadowed(e),
    expr_ultimate_type("client", e, encl),
    type_ancestor(encl, anc),
    di_field_in_type(anc, name, t).

// expr_type makes the concrete method findable; expr_exact_type makes
// call_exact_receiver true, which is what suppresses the CHA fan. Both are needed —
// either one alone leaves the edge set unchanged.
expr_type("client", e, t) :- di_recv_mono(e, t).
expr_exact_type(e, t) :- di_recv_mono(e, t).
